  "worked_solution": "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${y}^{2}>9$\" is the set $(-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (3,\\infty ),$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$y>0$\" is ${R}^{+}.$\n\nSince $A⇒B≡¬A∨B,$ the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P} & =\\overset{―}{(-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (3,\\infty )}\\cup {R}^{+} \\\\  & =[-3,3]\\cup {R}^{+} \\\\  & =[-3,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne R.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃y\\in Z,\\,P(y)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
